# Windows Services Not Running or Misconfigured ##

      ## Issue ##
- Certain Windows features or apps do not work.
- Errors appear stating a service is not running.
- System functions fail after updates or software installs.
- Services stop unexpectedly or do not start at boot.

      ## Cause  ## (Root cause or most likely cause)
- Required Windows services are disabled or set to the wrong startup type.
- Services were changed by updates, third-party software, or manual configuration.
- Dependency services are not running.
- Service account permissions are misconfigured.

            ## Steps / Resolution ##
1. Check service status
- Press: Win + R
- Type: services.msc
- Locate the service related to the issue.
- Check if the service is "Running" or "Stopped".

[Screenshot1.png]


 2. Verify startup type
- Right-click the service -> Properties
- Set Startup type to:
- "Automatic" (most system services)
- "Manual" (services started only when needed)

[Screenshot2.png]


3. Start or restart the service
- Right-click the service -> Start or Restart.


4. Check service dependencies
- Open Service Properties: Dependencies tab
- Ensure required dependency services are running.

[Screenshot3.png


 Dependencies Tab 
> Top box: Services this service depends on
- These must be running.
- Check and start these services first.

> Bottom box: Services that depend on this service
- These services may stop if this service stops.
- This section is informational and usually not part of troubleshooting.


 5. Reset service configuration
- Open **Command Prompt** or **PowerShell** as Administrator.
- Reconfigure startup type if needed (can also be done in `services.msc`).
Run system file repairs:'sfc /scannow"
> If issues persist: " DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth "


6. Restart the computer
- Ensures all service configuration changes apply correctly during boot.



      ##  Why It Happened & Why the Fix Worked ##
- Windows relies on background services to perform core system tasks.
- If a required service is disabled, stopped, or misconfigured, dependent features fail.
- Restarting the service and correcting the startup type restores functionality.
- Checking dependencies ensures required services are running.
- Repairing system files with SFC and DISM restores corrupted Windows components that services rely on.
